[check-e2e] KNOWN DEFECT sh: a ( ) group cannot be a pipeline stage
[check-e2e]   CHCK_GROUP (src/bin/sh.s) refuses a group with NCMD != 1, i.e. one that has a `|` on its line. This path does not go through RUN_PIPELINE, which is where a stage's fd 1 is wired to the pipe, and IGNORING that is the silent wrong answer docs/SH-FEATURES.md sec 17 rules against -- measured before the check existed, `( ECHO GPIPE ) | WC` printed GPIPE and no counts, the `|` simply vanishing. LOUD refusal, never a wrong answer; pinned rather than excluded because POSIX and V7 both allow it.

THIS ENTRY HAS NOW BEEN NARROWED TWICE IN ONE DAY, which is the part worth reading.

It first claimed a group was refused in any COMPOUND line, on the strength of `X ; ( A ) ; Y` producing nothing. That was a DIFFERENT defect and not a refusal at all: the group span was seeded only at token 0 of the LINE, and `;` does not split before TOKENIZE, so a `(` after a separator was tokenized as an ordinary word and the line silently misparsed -- CHCK_GROUP never saw a group. Fixed in sh.s; cases 118 and 126 went DEFECT-FIXED.

It then covered REDIRECTS too, on the reasoning that honouring `( ... ) > F` 'needs RUN_PIPELINE's staging'. Half true: it needs THE STAGING, not RUN_PIPELINE. Extracting STG_OUTFILE and INIT_CHLDMAP from BLD_FDCTS_STG made the identical routines reachable from SUB_GROUP for +25 bytes, and cases 116 and 117 went DEFECT-FIXED -- redirected and nested-redirected groups now agree with /bin/sh byte for byte, `2>&1` included.

So a pin's stated reason is a claim about the code, and two of the three claims this one made did not survive being checked. What is left is the pipeline half, which is genuinely different: the group would have to BE a stage, not merely carry a redirect. `< FILE` is refused separately and permanently -- a group's fd 0 is the body pipe the child sh reads its script from, so there is no fd 0 to redirect.
[check-e2e] KNOWN DEFECT sh: nested $( ) is refused
[check-e2e]   One level of command substitution works (this section's subst-1/2/4 pass); `$(ECHO $(ECHO DEEP))` is refused and writes no output file. SUB_TRKCH counts parens without knowing the child shell's quoting or nesting depth, the same machinery behind docs/TASKS.md #18(f)'s quoted-paren limit. Closing it costs bytes sh.bin does not have (16,332 of 16,384), so it is pinned here rather than fixed -- and pinned, not excluded, because the case is legal V7 and legal POSIX.
